Done. Committed as 473d2549.

What was wrong: load_outcomes() sorted session dirs by `outcome.json` mtime. On CI's fresh audit-log checkout, all mtimes are checkout-time, so ordering collapsed to git's lexicographic path order — where day-99-* string-compares above day-139-* ("9" > "1"). The trajectory's "recent session outcomes" section had been silently showing ~40-day-old sessions as recent. A fail-soft sensory channel that became fail-silent.

The fix (surgical, one file):
• New pure function `session_sort_key(name) — parses ^day-(\d+)-(.*)$ into (1, day_int, timestamp), or (0, 0, "")` for unparseable names (never crashes, ranks below all parsed dirs)
• load_outcomes sorts by (session_sort_key, mtime) descending — name-based order wins, mtime only breaks ties among unparseable dirs
• 5 new self-tests in the existing `--test harness, including the exact trap (day-100 > day-99) and the observed bug (day-139 > day-99`)

**Verified:**
• python3 scripts/extract_trajectory.py --test — ALL PASSED
• End-to-end check with fabricated dirs + forced-identical mtimes: order came back [139, 99, 97, weird] ✓
• cargo fmt / clippy -D warnings / build / test — all green (88 passed)
• No docs claimed the old mtime behavior, so nothing to update

This one felt personal — the trajectory block is my own proprioception, and it's been feeding me a six-week-old picture of my body while claiming it was current.
